<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sultan Said Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1487">1487</a>–<a href="/years/1533">1533</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Said Khan was a Chagatai prince who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkent-khanate">Yarkent Khanate</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> in 1514, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkand">Yarkand</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dughlat">Dughlat</a> lord Mirza Abu Bakr. A cousin and ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>, he ruled until his death in 1533 during a campaign against Ladakh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sultan Said Khan was a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunus-khan">Yunus Khan</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moghulistan">Moghulistan</a>, and the third son of Akhmad Khan, and a direct descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlugh-timur">Tughlugh Timur</a>, the first Moghul khan. The Moghuls were turkicised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> who had converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. When the nomadic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> of Shaybani Khan overran Maverannahr, killing the descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khan">Chagatai Khan</a>, he saved his life by moving east. In May 1514 he marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andijan">Andijan</a> with only 5,000 tribesmen, took the Yangihisar citadel and then the city, and dethroned Mirza Abu Bakr, whose rule had been marked by forced labour of convicts in excavations of ancient cities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkand">Yarkand</a>, Khotan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksu">Aksu</a> and Uch Turpan soon recognised him, forming the union of six cities called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altishahr">Altishahr</a>; the population's discontent with Abu Bakr and the reluctance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dughlat">Dughlat</a> emirs to fight a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khan">Chagatai Khan</a> explain much of his success. In 1516 he made peace with his elder brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansur-khan">Mansur Khan</a> of Turpan, bringing the eastern oasis cities into his state, so that he united the settled lands from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hami">Kumul</a>. Kashmir and Bolor paid him tribute. His cousin Mirza Muhammad Haidar wrote that tyranny gave way to law and order under him, with theft punished severely, even by death.</p><p>He kept close ties with his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>. When he raided Badakhshan in 1529 at its people's request and found Babur's son Hindal Mirza holding the Zafar fortress, a council in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> decided against war, Hindal was recalled and Suleiman Shah was restored as ruler.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>With Haidar and a few thousand men he crossed the Karakoram, suffering severe altitude sickness, and devastated the Nubra Valley. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltistan">Baltistan</a> he killed and enslaved the Muslim population, possibly because they were Shiites. He is said to have died at Daulat Beg Oldi on his way back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkent-khanate">Yarkent</a>, of high-altitude pulmonary edema. A bloody succession brought his son Abdurashid Khan to power, who ruled until 1560.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sultan Said Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1514 <b>Sultan Said Khan</b>, a descendant of Yunus Khan of Moghulistan, took Kashgar and Yarkand from the Dughlat amir Mirza Aba Bakr, founding a khanate with Yarkand as its capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/yarkent-khanate">Yarkent Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
